course: dictionary
lesson: 6258
title: Molehills and mountains
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A molehill is a small pile of earth pushed up by a mole digging its tunnels underground. «Moles dig tunnels and push the soil up into molehills» tells you where the earth comes from. Look for the word hill inside molehill: it is a little hill.
explain: Words can also be used in sayings. «Make a mountain out of a molehill» does not mean building a real mountain. It means treating a small problem as if it were huge. Ask yourself: is the writer talking about real earth, or about a problem that is too big for its size?
explain: Context clues help you here too. Catastrophising means jumping from a small thing to the worst possible ending, and making a mountain out of a molehill means making a small problem huge. Swap your meaning into the sentence: if "making a small problem huge" still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: A molehill is a small pile of earth pushed up by a mole digging underground.
fact: «Moles dig tunnels and push the soil up into molehills.»
fact: «Make a mountain out of a molehill» means treating a small problem as if it were huge.
fact: Someone who is overreacting is treating a small problem as a huge one.
fact: Other phrases to remember: «a field full of molehills» and «make a mountain out of a molehill».
exercise: What is a molehill? | *A small pile of earth pushed up by a mole digging underground | A mole's tunnel under a path | A hole where a rabbit hides | A tall hill covered in grass
exercise: Dad says, «Don't make a mountain out of a molehill, it's only a scratch.» What does he mean? | He is asking you to dig a hole | *You are overreacting to a small problem | The scratch is very deep and dangerous | He wants you to climb a real mountain
exercise: «Animals build with whatever they have: wasps chew wood into paper, moles push soil up into molehills, and ants pile pine needles into a warm dome.» Find and copy the word that names a mole's pile of earth. | *molehills | ants | wasps | paper
test: In the phrase «a field full of molehills», what would you find on the grass? | Ripe corn ready to pick | Sheep that have wandered off | *Small piles of earth made by moles | Tall trees growing in rows
test: Jess says, «It's just a spilt drink, you're making a mountain out of a molehill!» What is Jess saying? | *Your reaction is too big for such a small problem | Mountains are dangerous places to drink | You should bring a bigger cup | The drink is very tall and needs moving
exam: Which sentence shows someone treating a small problem as if it were huge? | *Mia wailed that losing one sock had ruined her whole life | Tom climbed the hill to see the view | The wasps built a paper nest in the shed | The mole dug a long tunnel under the field
